n\n### 1. Robert Hood – Minimal Nation / minimal techno philosophy\n\n#### a) The Quietus – “Love Is The Message: An Interview With Robert Hood” (2013)\n\n- **Outlet:** The Quietus  \n- **Focus:** Minimal Nation, minimalism, spiritual/artistic grounding  \n\n**Quotable sentences:**\n\nFrom the 1994 Minimal Nation sleevenotes, reproduced in the interview:\n\n> “The first key to understanding minimalism is to realise that it emanates from art. Art emanates from vision, vision from purpose and purpose from the creator, God… Minimal Nation is not just a collection of rhythm tracks, but a supernatural work of art realised.”[6]\n\n> “I felt a strong sense of urgency to create a body of work that identified with what was placed on my heart, as opposed to what was on my mind.”[6]\n\n- **URL:** `https://thequietus.com/interviews/robert-hood-interview-floorplan-paradise`[6]\n\n---\n\n#### b) The Quietus – “Fountain Of Living Water: The Strange World Of Robert Hood” (2024)\n\n- **Outlet:** The Quietus  \n- **Focus:** Hood’s broader discography and ongoing view of minimalism  \n\n**Quotable sentences:**\n\n> “Minimalism’s not about stripping down the track, it’s getting emotion from the smallest part.”[14]\n\n> “The idea was to make just diminutive music, just basic rhythm tracks with a spiritual seed, to look at each track as a seed so that each would grow and produce a harvest, to get away from rave culture and plant these minimalist art renderings in black and white.”[14]\n\n- **URL:** `https://thequietus.com/interviews/strange-world-of/robert-hood-interview-discography`[14]\n\n---\n\n#### c) The Skinny – “Rhythm, Roots And Spirituality: Connecting The Dots With Robert Hood” (2014)\n\n- **Outlet:** The Skinny  \n- **Focus:** Explicit definition of minimal, trance, hidden rhythms  \n\n**Quotable sentences:**\n\n> “Minimal is artistic expression,” he states assertively. “It’s diminutive, simple artistic expression and to me it’s like a place of solitude.”[2]\n\n> “It’s not just about [using] a kick drum, a hi-hat, a bassline or some Morse code sound, for the sake of being minimal. It’s about identifying with your soul and with your spirit and finding a way to convey that and express that to whoever is listening.”[2]\n\n> “It’s about finding rhythm inside rhythms. If you listen closely to some of those tracks, such as The Rhythm of Vision or Unix and Station Rider E, you sort of find other hidden rhythms inside of the rhythm.”[2]\n\n> “It can be very hypnotic. To me that’s the true trance music; you find yourself really lost in it.”[2]\n\n- **URL:** `https://www.theskinny.co.uk/clubs/interviews/rhythm-roots-and-spirituality-connecting-the-dots-with-robert-hood`[2]\n\n---\n\n#### d) Gridface – “Robert Hood: In the Spirit” (2010)\n\n- **Outlet:** Gridface (long-form interview; artist-friendly site)  \n- **Focus:** Coining “minimal,” naming Minimal Nation, repetition  \n\n**Quotable sentences:**\n\n> “As it applies to techno, yeah. I hadn’t heard anybody use that term.”[3]\n\n> “I was going to call it something like ‘access-authorized repetition.’ Something repetitive.”[3]\n\n> “I told Jeff that the minimal nation is rising, and he said, ‘That’s it.’”[3]\n\n- **URL:** `https://www.gridface.com/robert-hood-interview/`[3]\n\n---\n\n#### e) Groove (Germany) – “11. ROBERT HOOD Minimal Nation (Axis, 1994)” (2014)\n\n- **Outlet:** Groove (German; includes direct quotes, some in German)  \n- **Focus:** Reaction to rave, reduced sound, soul, hypnotic / trance  \n\n**Quotable sentences (German):**\n\n> “Ich sehnte mich nach einem reduzierten und minimalistischen Sound.”[12]\n\n> “Ich wollte einen heruntergestrippten Sound, der nicht so überproduziert wirkt, aber gleichzeitig ein Maximum an Gefühl und Seele transportiert.”[12]\n\n> “Das hat einen spirituellen, hypnotischen Moment für mich.”[12]\n\n> “Für mich persönlich war das die Essenz von Trance-Musik.”[12]\n\n- **URL:** `https://groove.de/2014/02/03/die-besten-elektronischen-alben-platz-11-robert-hood-minimal-nation-1994/`[12]\n\n---\n\n#### f) Fader / later synthesis (for context, not a full primary interview)\n\nMultiple pieces (including RA and Fader) are quoted in retrospectives:\n\n> Hood described his style as “minimum structure” and “maximum soul.”[1][4]\n\n> “Once I had that chord sound and a particular pattern I realized I didn’t need anything else.”[1][5]\n\n- “minimum structure / maximum soul” and the Juno-2 anecdote are widely repeated; if you need strict primary sourcing, trace back to **Fader (2013)** and RA’s 2009 *Minimal Nation* reissue review/interview.[1][5]\n\n---\n\n### 2. Basic Channel / Moritz von Oswald / Mark Ernestus – repetition, space, dub\n\nDirect, long interviews where they analytically unpack “repetition” and “space” in Basic Channel-era techno are relatively rare and often brief; much of the discourse is by critics, not the duo themselves. Below are citable items.\n\n#### a) Red Bull Music Academy – Moritz von Oswald Lecture “Early Morning Freestyles” (2015)\n\n- **Outlet:** Red Bull Music Academy  \n- **Focus:** Career overview, Basic Channel, minimal techno, dub  \n\nThe RBMA page’s editorial intro summarises:\n\n> “Having practically invented minimal techno with Basic Channel, they then moved into the twilight world of dub, creating the Rhythm & Sound alias to explore the spaces beyond the bassline.”[8]\n\n- **URL:** `https://www.redbullmusicacademy.com/lectures/moritz-von-oswald-early-morning-freestyles`[8]\n\nFor academic work, note this line is editorial RBMA copy rather than a word-for-word quote from Moritz himself, but it succinctly ties Basic Channel, minimalism, dub and “spaces”.\n\n---\n\n#### b) self-titled magazine – “A Quick Talk With Moritz Von Oswald…” (2012)\n\n- **Outlet:** self-titled  \n- **Focus:** Process; how concepts emerge from sound  \n\n**Quotable sentences:**\n\n> “Well, it isn’t a concept so much as whatever comes out of the [music] we’re working on at the time. The sound dictates the concept.”[7]\n\n> “[With Basic Channel and Rhythm & Sound] we didn’t sit down and say, ‘Okay, let’s try this.’ We worked on the music first, and then the artwork, and the label ideas–how we would present everything to the public.”[7]\n\n- **URL:** `https://www.self-titledmag.com/a-quick-talk-with-moritz-von-oswald-about-getting-deep-with-dubstep-the-richness-of-the-next-moritz-von-oswald-trio-record-and-why-minimal-techno-isnt-as-cold-as-you-think/`[7]\n\nThis interview is more about process and the lack of a fixed “concept” than an explicit breakdown of repetition/space, but it’s still useful for a philosophy-of-work angle.\n\n---\n\n#### c) Rhythm & Sound interview – The Wire 236 (October 2003)\n\n- **Outlet:** The Wire (as archived; text you saw is likely an excerpt/summary, not a full verbatim transcript)  \n\nThe line reproduced in the archive:\n\n> “The use of repetition sometimes threatens to tickle the boredom threshold, but the listener tends to bounce off it and sink into the ample folds of the mix.”[9]\n\n- **URL (archival transcription):** `https://teletype.in/@alxpsr/rhythmnsoundwire`[9]\n\nNote: This sentence reads like The Wire author’s description rather than a quote from Ernestus/von Oswald. Check the original print article if you need strictly artist-spoken lines; otherwise it’s a strong critical formulation of their repetition/dub aesthetic.\n\n---\n\n#### d) Contextual/secondary sources on “place,” space and dub\n\nWhile not interviews, these capture the philosophy in words you can quote, if you’re comfortable citing critical rather than artist voices.\n\n- **Monumental Movement column – “Basic Channel ── The origin of Berlin dub techno”**\n\n> “In other words, music is not a material but a ‘place’ – this philosophy later defined Dub Techno.”[11]\n\n> “The end of a rhythm becomes the starting point of the next reverberation - this cyclical structure forms the basis of their sound.”[11]\n\n- **URL:** `https://monumental-movement.jp/en/column-basic-channel/`